// twosided-demo — a book set for printing on both sides of the sheet.
//
// A two-sided document is read as openings rather than as a stack of pages, and the two halves of
// an opening are mirror images of each other: the binding margin is on the right of a verso and on
// the left of a recto. `\geometry twoside:on` says so, and everything else here follows from it —
// the margins mirror, the folio moves to the outer corner, the running head names the chapter on
// one side of the opening and the section on the other, and each chapter opens on a recto, taking
// a blank leaf when it has to.
//
// Print it double-sided, short-edge off, and hold it as a book: the wide margin stays against the
// spine on every leaf, and no folio ever falls into the gutter.
\use{book}

// inner and outer name the margins the way the reader sees them, not the way the sheet does. The
// inner margin is deliberately the wider of the two — it is the one the binding eats into — and
// naming either of them makes the document two-sided without a separate twoside:on.
\geometry paper:a5 inner:24mm outer:15mm top:18mm bottom:22mm

\title{Two Sides of the Sheet}
\author{A Demonstration}
\date{Set with texish}

// ===== Front matter =============================================================
// Roman folios, unnumbered chapters. The parity that mirrors the margins is the folio's, so the
// front matter's i, ii, iii mirror exactly as the main matter's 1, 2, 3 do.
\frontmatter
\maketitle

\chapter{About this booklet}

Hold an opening of a printed book and the two pages are not the same shape. The margin against
the spine is narrower to the eye than it measures, because the binding curves part of it away,
and the margin at the outer edge carries the reader's thumb. A page laid out once and printed on
both sides gets this wrong on every second leaf: the gutter margin lands at the outer edge and
the book reads as though it were falling off its own pages.

The remedy is as old as printing. Lay out one side and reflect it for the other, so that the
frame is described by its relation to the binding — inner and outer — rather than by left and
right, which change places every time the leaf is turned.

// ===== Main matter ==============================================================
// Arabic folios restarting at 1. \mainmatter's \eject settles the folio; the chapter that follows
// opens on a recto, inserting a blank leaf first if the next page would have been a verso.
\mainmatter

\chapter{The frame}

\section{Inner and outer}

The frame given to \mono{\\geometry} is the recto's, and every verso reflects it about the page's
vertical centre. The reflection carries the running head and the running foot with the body, so a
folio set against the outer edge stays outer on both sides of the opening rather than swapping to
the gutter on every second page.

Because it is the resolved frame that reflects, and not a named pair of margins, any geometry is
two-sided the moment it is asked to be: a text block positioned by one edge and a width mirrors
exactly as a pair of margins does.

The measurements are worth stating plainly, because they are the whole of the feature. These pages
are A5, and the text block is 24 millimetres from the binding and 15 from the outer edge — on a
recto, 24 on the left and 15 on the right, and on a verso the reverse. Lay two facing pages
side by side and the pair is symmetrical about the fold: 15, then text, then 24 and 24 with the
fold between them, then text, then 15. That symmetry is what the eye reads as a well-set opening,
and it is invisible until the sheet is printed on both sides and folded.

The alternative — one frame for every page — is what a word processor gives, and it is why a
double-sided print from one so often has to be re-run with a wider left margin, then re-run again
because the right-hand pages now look wrong. There is no margin that is correct for both sides,
which is exactly why the frame has to know which side it is on.

\section{Which side a page is on}

A page is a recto when its folio is odd. That is the same parity the margins mirror on, and the
same one \mono{\\cleardoublepage} tests, so a front matter renumbered to restart the body at one
keeps the margins, the blank leaves and the printed folios all agreeing with each other.

The \mono{document} format exposes the test as \mono{\\ifrecto}, which takes its first branch on a
recto and its second on a verso. Every folio in this booklet is placed with it: one
definition, two placements, chosen as each page ships rather than written out twice.

Reading the parity from the folio rather than from a count of sheets matters more than it sounds.
A book's front matter is numbered separately — i, ii, iii — and restarts at 1 where the body
begins, so the two counts part company early and never meet again. A frame mirrored on the sheet
count would drift out of step with the folios the reader can actually see, and the drift would
show up as a gutter margin on the wrong side halfway through the front matter, which is a hard
defect to explain and an easy one to ship.

The same parity drives the blank leaves, so the three mechanisms — the mirrored frame, the folio,
and the leaf a chapter opens on — are answers from one source and cannot disagree.

\section{What the reflection carries}

The head and the foot are reflected along with the body, and this is the part that is easy to get
half right. A folio pushed to one end of the running foot is not at a fixed distance from the left
edge of the sheet; it is at the end of the text block, and the text block has moved. Reflecting the
block without the material around it leaves a folio marching down the outer edge of the rectos and
the inner edge of the versos, which is worse than leaving it centred.

So the whole frame moves as one, and a foot written once — the folio at the far end on a recto and
at the near end on a verso — comes out at the outer corner of every page in the book. The head is
placed the same way, which is why the chapter and section names in this booklet sit centred over a
text block that is itself in two different places depending on the leaf.

Turn to any opening in the middle of a chapter and the effect is there to read: the verso carries
the chapter's title and the recto the title of whichever section is running through it. Neither
page knows anything about the other. Each simply reports the marks that reached it, and the pair
reads as an opening because the two streams were written to say different things to begin with.

None of this is a special case bolted onto the page builder. A mark is an ordinary item in the
vertical list, invisible and unbreakable, and the page builder records which marks a page carried
as it ships. Adding a second stream adds a second set of those records and nothing else: no new
page-breaking rule, no ordering constraint between the streams, and no way for one to interfere
with the other. That is worth more than the feature itself, because a running head that reads
correctly on ordinary pages and then goes wrong on a page with a float, a footnote or a page break
in an awkward place is not a running head anyone can trust.

The engine's own contribution is equally small. It mirrors one number — the offset of the text
block from the left edge of the sheet — and the header and footer follow because they are placed
relative to that block rather than to the sheet. Everything the reader sees as two-sided design
comes out of one reflection and two mark streams.

\chapter{The opening}

\section{A head read across two pages}

A running head in a two-sided book is not a per-page caption but a pair. The verso names the
chapter and the recto the section within it, so a reader glancing at an opening is told both where
they are and how far into it they have come. Two independent mark streams carry the two: a chapter
writes the first with \mono{\\mark} and a section the second with \mono{\\submark}.

A chapter clears the section stream as it opens, so the recto of an opening that has not reached
its first section yet carries no head at all — better than naming the last section of the chapter
before, which is what a single stream would leave behind.

Which of the two a head shows depends only on which side of the fold its page falls. A single
stream cannot do this: it would have to choose between naming the chapter on both sides, which
wastes half the head, or naming the section on both, which loses the reader's place the moment a
chapter runs past its first section.

The streams are ordinary marks in the vertical list, so they travel with the text rather than
with the page. A section that begins two thirds of the way down a page is the section that page's
head names, and the section running over onto the next page is the one that page names, because
each page reports the marks that actually landed on it.

\section{Opening on the right}

A chapter belongs on a recto: the reader turns the leaf and the new chapter is there, whole, on
the right-hand page. When the previous chapter happens to end on a recto, that means a blank leaf
in between — and a genuinely blank one, carrying neither running head nor folio, which is what
the blank verso of a printed book looks like.

A page that is blank except for a running head and a page number is not blank; it is a page that
looks like a mistake. Printers have suppressed both on inserted leaves for as long as books have
been made in signatures, and a document that does not is announcing an accident it did not have.

One-sided, none of this applies, and the format does not pretend otherwise: a chapter takes the
next page, no leaf is inserted, the head names the chapter on every page and the folio sits in the
middle of the foot. The same document set both ways gives two correct books, not one correct book
and one with the two-sided machinery showing through.

\chapter{A short chapter}

This chapter is short enough to end where it began, which is the case that costs a leaf: the next
chapter cannot start on the very next page without landing on a verso.

\chapter{And the next}

Turn back one leaf from this page in the printed copy and the blank verso is there — an intended
blank, not a mistake, and the reason a printed book's page count is so often a multiple of four.
